---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: {{ app_service_account }}
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
  labels:
    auth-method: {{ auth_method }}
{% if auth_method == 'iamra' %}
---
{#
  The ServiceAccount is the security boundary for the iamra path, not just an
  identity. Because the CSI driver runs with useTokenRequest: true, the
  certificate is requested AS this ServiceAccount, so "may this workload obtain a
  certificate, and therefore assume the matching AWS role?" is answered here.

  Drop this Role and the pod cannot get a certificate at all -- which is the
  behaviour you want. It is also worth confirming no broad cluster-wide grant on
  certificaterequests already exists:

    oc get clusterrolebindings -o json \
      | jq -r '.items[] | select(.roleRef.name|test("cert-manager")) | .metadata.name'
#}
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: {{ app_service_account }}-certificaterequests
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["cert-manager.io"]
    resources: ["certificaterequests"]
    verbs: ["create", "get", "list", "watch", "delete"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: {{ app_service_account }}-certificaterequests
  namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: Role
  name: {{ app_service_account }}-certificaterequests
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_service_account }}
    namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
---
{# Referencing a cluster-scoped AWSPCAClusterIssuer requires read access to it. #}
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: iamra-pca-issuer-reader
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["awspca.cert-manager.io"]
    resources: ["awspcaclusterissuers"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
    resourceNames: ["{{ cluster_issuer_name }}"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: iamra-pca-issuer-reader-{{ app_namespace }}-{{ app_service_account }}
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: iamra-pca-issuer-reader
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: {{ app_service_account }}
    namespace: {{ app_namespace }}
{% endif %}
{#
  oidc needs no extra RBAC at all: the pod's identity IS the projected token, and
  kubelet mints it without any permission on the workload's part. The boundary is
  entirely on the AWS side, in the role's sub/aud condition.

  vault needs none either. The pod presents its ServiceAccount token to Vault;
  Vault does the TokenReview itself using its own auth-delegator permission, and
  the boundary is the bound_service_account_names/namespaces on the Vault role.

  Both are, on this axis, simpler than iamra -- there is no in-cluster
  permission to get wrong.
#}
